Dolby audio for Ruby
===================

This tree selects LunarisDolby, Pong's DAP tuning and the complete 64-bit
DAX3_3.7.0.8_r1 backend, including its real Android Spatializer and Codec2
AC-3, E-AC-3/JOC and AC-4 decoders. The pinned upstream is
[Pong Development](https://github.com/Pong-Development/hardware_dolby) at
`75c39aca2d6f3392519d65da0e6823217c8297ad`.
All 16 native libraries and the DMS executable retain their upstream bytes;
`proprietary/provenance.json` records their SHA-256 identities.

Integration
-----------

Inherit `hardware/dolby/dolby.mk` and include
`media_codecs_dolby_audio.xml` from the device's vendor Codec2 XML. Remove
duplicate device Dolby modules, app, XML tuning and policy. DMS and the
source Codec2 host install their own init and VINTF fragments. The Codec2
store uses HIDL 1.2 instance `default1`, alongside the existing MediaTek store.
The host uses current HAL headers instead of the upstream prebuilt wrapper.
The existing Lineage `libstagefright_foundation-v33.vendor` compatibility
module is used without modifying framework sources.

MediaTek's primary HAL and microphone preprocessing remain in one 32-bit
process. `android.hardware.audio.service.rubyx` retains the stock core,
SoundTrigger and Bluetooth registration paths and exposes a composite
`IEffectsFactory/default`. Only the four known Dolby software-insert UUIDs
are forwarded to the 64-bit `IEffectsFactory/dolby` companion. Other effect
objects and raw primary-HAL effect IDs stay local. Remote IEffect Binder
objects are returned directly, so PCM buffers and FMQs reach the companion
without a second processing proxy. Companion death restarts the core HAL
and AudioFlinger through the normal init recovery path.

Both effect implementation architectures are required. The extra `dolby`
instance must be included in the device manifest and framework compatibility
matrix; the provided modules and makefile do this. Append device matrix
paths with `+=` so they retain this matrix. Keep the transferred public,
private and vendor Dolby SELinux policy together.

The device audio policy must expose software Spatializer output profiles
and routes. HIDL 7.0 uses the exact FAST + DEEP_BUFFER flag combination;
current AudioPolicy maps it to SPATIALIZER, then maps it back at the HAL
boundary. Ruby's profiles use a stereo hardware sink; AudioFlinger supplies
the multichannel mixer input to the Spatializer. A2DP has a separate profile
in the device's copy of the Bluetooth policy. Normal outputs remain present.

The shared effects XML retains normal effects and AEC/NS/AGC and adds the
real Spatializer UUID. `libdlbvol` ships as upstream does; its offload volume
listeners are deliberately not registered or delegated as software effects.
Dolby OMX implementations are removed. Compatibility aliases name the
Codec2 decoders and do not install an OMX service.

Tuning and controller
---------------------

The Pong DAX file is installed to both vendor and ODM because DMS prefers
ODM when that path exists. Device-specific Xiaomi DAX files are removed.
The Spatializer uses its compiled generic profiles with
`ro.vendor.audio.dolby.spatial.xml=false`; no absent optional XML or different
device identity is advertised. Speaker transaural spatialization defaults
off. Hardware identity and existing user data are preserved.

Lunaris owns the controller UI. Its process shares a lazily created native
effect across repository clients, handles initialization failure, restores
saved settings after reconnection and closes clients with their lifecycle.
Settings summaries allocate no native effect. Initial enable defaults to
true; a saved user choice wins. Invalid saved profiles fall back to profile
0. Only explicitly saved controls override Pong's profile tuning. Optional
app-profile polling stops while the screen is off.

Validation
----------

Static source, XML, blob identity, native symbol and dependency checks do
not establish successful build or playback. No build is run by this change.
The maintainer must verify on a new build:

1. Core32, effects64, DMS and Codec2 `default1` register and stay alive.
   There is one controller and one owner for each service/configuration.
2. Codec2 enumerates and actually decodes all four MIME types, including
   multichannel JOC. The MediaTek video store remains available.
3. `dumpsys media.audio_policy`, `media.audio_flinger` and `audio` show the
   Spatializer effect, output profile and active route. Test wired/USB and
   Bluetooth A2DP separately. Check buffer transport and underruns.
4. Lunaris opens, profiles/EQ/toggles work, and boot, service recovery and a
   dirty upgrade preserve usable state. Test calls and microphone AEC/NS/AGC.
5. Apple Music exposes Atmos and actually selects an Atmos track on the
   intended route. Capture logs and codec/Spatializer dumps during playback.

The decoder still rejects its internal Atmos Music processing profile 4.
This is a separate namespace from Lunaris/DMS profile 4. It is not bypassed
or relabeled. Whether Apple Music requests that internal mode, and whether
its eligibility logic accepts this device, remain runtime questions. Do not
report an Atmos badge or working Atmos playback based on XML/properties alone.

The earlier multilib candidate and its ARM32 stack-unwind repair are retained
in backup history. That affected library and repair tool are not shipped by
this complete Pong64 integration.
